<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Recycle Our Paper! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> An Environmental Project</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Recycle Our Paper!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Extract practical information from simple instructional videos.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can extract specific information from instructional videos about ways to keep the environment clean (e.g., 'Turn off the lights to save energy.').<br>Can ask for questions to clarify spoken instructions when working collaboratively (e.g., 'Put plastic in this bin.' 'Do you mean plastic bottles or all types of plastic?')<br>Can show comprehension of specific spoken instructions in environmental projects after asking for repetition (e.g., 'Can you repeat that, please?' 'Yes, put that paper in this bin.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher will display pictures of different types of waste (paper, plastic, glass, food scraps) and a picture of a clean classroom and a polluted park. Students will listen to the teacher's questions and point to the correct image. E.g., Teacher asks: "Which picture shows a clean environment?" (Students point). "Which picture shows paper waste?" (Students point).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher will hold up a piece of paper and say, "This is paper. We can recycle paper." The teacher will then pick up a plastic bottle and say, "This is plastic. We need to clean plastic." The teacher will model the pronunciation of key vocabulary words like 'recycle,' 'paper,' 'environment,' and 'clean.' The teacher will also introduce and model the pronunciation of 'will' /wɪl/ in sentences like, "We will recycle paper." and 'I'll' /aɪl/ in "I'll bring the paper." Students repeat chorally and individually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we recycle a banana peel?" (Expected answer: "No.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a plan to clean the environment?" (Showing a picture of people planting trees.) (Expected answer: "Yes.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "An Environmental Project".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short (1.5-2 minutes) simple instructional video titled "How to Recycle Paper in Our Classroom." The video will show children demonstrating the steps: collecting paper, sorting it, and putting it into a blue recycling bin. It will use simple imperatives and present simple sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can understand simple instructions and factual information about everyday environmental actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> Before playing the video, the teacher asks: "What is the video about?" (Expected answers: recycling, paper, cleaning). Students listen for the main idea.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students listen to the video for a second time. The teacher provides simple True/False statements based on the video's instructions. E.g., "We put plastic in the paper bin." (False). "We put paper in the blue bin." (True). "We will clean the classroom." (True - linking to the project).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching Activity:</b> The teacher projects images representing key actions from the video (e.g., "collecting paper," "sorting paper," "putting paper in bin"). Students listen to sentences from the video (e.g., "Collect all the paper," "Sort the paper," "Put the paper in the blue bin.") and point to the matching image.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for key vocabulary (project, environment, classroom, plan, paper, clean, bring) and grammatical structures (imperatives for instructions: 'Bring the paper', 'Clean your desk'; future simple for plans: 'We will plant trees', 'I'll bring the supplies'). Focus on the pronunciation of 'will' /wɪl/ and 'I'll' /aɪl/, and segmenting multisyllabic words like 'presentation'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Listen and Complete:</b> The teacher plays audio snippets of simple instructions related to the environmental project (e.g., "______ the paper," "We ______ plant trees tomorrow," "I'll ______ the blue bin to the classroom.") and students listen and write the missing verb or noun from a word bank (e.g., collect, will, bring, clean). This reinforces imperatives and future simple.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Listen and Circle:</b> The teacher reads sentences with similar-sounding words or words with target pronunciation. For example, "We will clean the *classroom*." vs "We will clean the *garden*." Students listen and circle the correct word they hear on a worksheet. This activity also focuses on discriminating 'will' from other words, and segmenting multisyllabic words like 'environment' and 'presentation' if included in the sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Listening and Drawing:</b> In pairs, one student (A) listens to the teacher give a simple instruction (e.g., "Draw a paper in the bin." "Draw a plant in the garden.") and draws it. Their partner (B) listens to A giving a similar instruction and draws. Then, they compare drawings and provide feedback. This incorporates pragmatic functions of giving instructions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "An Environmental Project".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Group Listening and Action Planning.</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher plays a new, very short (30-45 seconds) audio recording of someone giving instructions for another part of an environmental project (e.g., "How to prepare the soil for plants." using imperatives like "Dig a hole," "Put the plant in," "Water the plant," and future simple like "We will care for our plants.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Each group listens to the audio twice. Then, they discuss and list the main instructions they heard. Students must actively use target vocabulary (e.g., 'plant', 'dig', 'water', 'care', 'project', 'garden').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher encourages students to ask for clarification if needed, fulfilling the learning outcome: "Do you mean a small hole or a big hole?" or "Can you repeat 'dig' please?" Groups then share their summarized instructions with the class. This stage promotes collaborative listening and discussion of environmental actions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher will use an observation checklist during Stage 4 to assess students' listening comprehension and interaction. The checklist will include items such as:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies at least 2 key instructions from the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., 'plant', 'dig', 'water') correctly in discussion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asks for clarification or repetition when needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates effectively in group discussion.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will listen to two final, simple instructions from the teacher related to the project (e.g., "Bring your empty water bottles tomorrow." and "We will clean the field next week.") and draw a simple picture representing each instruction on an index card. This demonstrates comprehension of specific spoken instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b> Intelligible drawing that clearly represents the instruction (for specific instruction comprehension), use of target vocabulary (verbally during discussion), clarity of questions asked (during group activity), and collaborative interaction (observed during group work).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I learned today is: __________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One way I can help recycle paper is: __________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to understand the instructions in English today? Why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing we can do as a class to improve our recycling project?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What listening strategies worked best for students (e.g., visuals, repetition)? Were the video and audio inputs at the appropriate CEFR level (A2.1)? How well did students use the target vocabulary and grammatical structures (imperatives, future simple)? What adjustments are needed to scaffold listening comprehension for specific students? For the next lesson (Reading), I will need to ensure the reading text includes the vocabulary introduced today and provides context for the specific actions we discussed for the environmental project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson will focus on reading comprehension, using a simple text about recycling or an environmental project, incorporating the vocabulary introduced today, to deepen understanding of the theme "Let's Recycle Our Paper!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>